Multisampled fragment shaders on Apple GPUs: each invocation is a subgroup
of its own

Godot 4's Forward+ renderer builds its light clusters by drawing each light's
volume in a pass with four samples per pixel whose fragment shader marks the
tiles and depth slices the light reaches. To save atomic writes it has one
invocation of each subgroup write for the others:

    uint first = subgroupBroadcastFirst(cluster_offset);
    mask = subgroupBallot(first == cluster_offset);
    ...
    if (subgroupBallotExclusiveBitCount(mask) == 0)
        atomicOr(cluster_render.data[...], bit);

On Apple GPUs a multisampled fragment function runs threads whose buffer
writes Metal discards, and nothing tells them from the others:
simd_is_helper_thread() is false for them, their coverage mask is not empty
and they take part in SIMD-group ballots. When such a thread is the first of
its group the write is lost, the tile never learns of the light and is drawn
unlit: black squares that flicker on lit surfaces
(https://github.com/godotengine/godot/issues/104168, and #121201 for Godot's
own Metal driver, which Godot 4.8 fixes for macOS builds only; a Windows
build under Wine runs the code above).

Measured on an Apple M5 Ultra (macOS 27.2) with a Metal program that draws 24
light volumes as Godot does and compares the tiles marked with those marked
when every thread writes: with four samples the shader above lost 9% of the
light bits, some in every one of 300 runs; with one sample none. A static
Godot 4.6.3 scene of 24 lights under Wine showed tiles missing a light in 78%
of its frames (80% on Godot 4.7.2); with this patch every frame is the same
picture.

With this patch a fragment shader of a multisampled pipeline is translated
with subgroups of one invocation (SPIRV-Cross's emulate_subgroups, which
patches/spirv-cross extends to every subgroup operation), so each invocation
writes for itself. Which invocations share a subgroup is the
implementation's choice in Vulkan; gl_SubgroupSize reads 1 in these shaders.

diff --git a/MoltenVK/MoltenVK/GPUObjects/MVKPipeline.mm b/MoltenVK/MoltenVK/GPUObjects/MVKPipeline.mm
index 7eae64c..f68e421 100644
--- a/MoltenVK/MoltenVK/GPUObjects/MVKPipeline.mm
+++ b/MoltenVK/MoltenVK/GPUObjects/MVKPipeline.mm
@@ -1686,6 +1686,19 @@ static void setEmulatedReversedDepthViewportConfig(SPIRVToMSLConversionConfigura
 			}
 #endif
 			shaderConfig.options.mslOptions.force_sample_rate_shading = pCreateInfo->pMultisampleState->sampleShadingEnable && pCreateInfo->pMultisampleState->minSampleShading != 0.0f;
+
+			/* Meridian: on Apple GPUs a multisampled fragment function runs SIMD-group threads whose
+			 * buffer writes Metal discards, although simd_is_helper_thread() is false for them and
+			 * their coverage mask is not empty. A shader that has one invocation of a subgroup
+			 * write for all of it loses the write when such a thread is the one chosen. Godot's light
+			 * cluster pass does that (subgroupBroadcastFirst, subgroupBallot, then atomicOr by the first
+			 * invocation, in a 4x pass) and leaves black tiles on lit surfaces (godotengine/godot#104168).
+			 * With one sample no write is lost. So in a multisampled pipeline each fragment invocation
+			 * is a subgroup of its own. */
+			if (getPhysicalDevice()->getMTLDeviceCapabilities().isAppleGPU &&
+				pCreateInfo->pMultisampleState->rasterizationSamples != VK_SAMPLE_COUNT_1_BIT) {
+				shaderConfig.options.mslOptions.emulate_subgroups = true;
+			}
 		}
 		if (std::any_of(shaderOutputs.begin(), shaderOutputs.end(), [](const SPIRVShaderOutput& output) { return output.builtin == spv::BuiltInLayer; })) {
 			shaderConfig.options.mslOptions.arrayed_subpass_input = true;
